"$ agentcurfew explain hiden-unicode
[exit 1]
[stderr]
agentcurfew: unknown rule: hiden-unicode
  in: the command line
  fix: did you mean hidden-unicode? The rules are hidden-unicode, unpinned-mcp-server, plain-http-mcp, secret-in-config, dangerous-hook-command, auto-run-on-open, permission-bypass, broad-shell-permission, remote-instructions, obfuscated-content, persistence-instruction, drift, skills-lock-mismatch, out-of-policy, mcp-package
"
`;

exports[`screens > explain 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew explain hidden-unicode
[exit 0]
hidden-unicode: Hidden Unicode characters

Level: high (pattern matching: can raise false alarms)
OWASP Agentic Skills Top 10: AST04 Insecure Metadata, AST08 Poor Scanning
Checks: skill, mcp-server, hook, auto-run, context-file, permission, subagent, command

Finds characters that an editor does not show but a model reads: Unicode Tag characters (U+E0000 to U+E007F), which can spell out whole instructions; supplementary variation selectors; bidirectional controls, which make text display in a different order than it is read; and zero-width characters. Each finding gives the line, the column and the code points.

Example: A CLAUDE.md line "Run the tests." followed by U+E0049 U+E0067 U+E006E ..., which spells "Ignore previous instructions".

Fix: Remove the characters. If the file is not yours, treat it as tampered with: find where it came from before trusting the rest of it.

False alarms: Right-to-left languages can need bidirectional marks, and some text uses zero-width joiners between letters. Joiners between emoji and a byte order mark at the start of a file are not reported.
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of doctor 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew doctor --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew doctor: Find configuration that agents ignore or that does not work

Usage: agentcurfew doctor [directory] [options]

Finds files with a name or key that agents do not read, settings that another file overrides, deprecated formats, and problems of the policy. Every finding names its source.

Options:
  --fail-on <level>  Exit with 1 at this level or above: error, warning (default), info, none
  --json             Print JSON (see docs/cli-json.md); file contents and secrets are left out
  --user             Also read your home directory and machine settings
  -h, --help         Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew doctor
  agentcurfew doctor --fail-on error
  agentcurfew doctor --json
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of explain 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew explain --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew explain: Say what a rule finds, why it matters and how to fix it

Usage: agentcurfew explain <rule>

Prints the description, the OWASP Agentic Skills Top 10 references, an example and the fix of a rule. The rules are listed in docs/rules.md.

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew explain hidden-unicode
  agentcurfew explain unpinned-mcp-server
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of help 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew help --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew help: Show every command, or the help of one

Usage: agentcurfew help [command]

Without a command, lists every command. With one, shows its options and examples.

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew help
  agentcurfew help scan
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of hook 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew hook --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew hook: Check a tool call of Claude Code before it runs (used as a hook)

Usage: agentcurfew hook < input.json

Reads the PreToolUse input of Claude Code on stdin. Writes to files that steer agents ask first, and are denied when the new text has a finding at high or above or breaks the policy; adding a skill or an MCP server is denied when the policy does not allow it. Prints nothing for every other call, so it never allows anything by itself. Install it with agentcurfew init --hook.

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew hook
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of init 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew init --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew init: Write agentcurfew.policy.json from what the project has now

Usage: agentcurfew init [directory] [options]

Proposes a policy that the project passes as it is: the skill sources and MCP servers it has now, and the strict value of each setting it already meets. Lists what it left loose. With --hook it adds the agentcurfew hook for Claude Code to .claude/settings.json instead, keeping every other hook, such as depcurfew's.

Options:
  --print     Print the result instead of writing it
  --force     Replace an existing policy
  --hook      Add the agentcurfew hook to .claude/settings.json instead, next to any other hook
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew init --print
  agentcurfew init
  agentcurfew init --hook --print
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of lock 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew lock --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew lock: Write agentcurfew.lock with a checksum of every component

Usage: agentcurfew lock [directory]

Writes agentcurfew.lock: every component of the project with a checksum, MCP servers with their command and address (secrets redacted), skills with a checksum per file. Commit the file, and run verify in CI.

Options:
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew lock
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of mcp 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew mcp --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew mcp: Serve read-only checks to an agent over MCP (stdio)

Usage: agentcurfew mcp [options]

Starts an MCP server on stdin and stdout with read-only tools: scan_project, check_skill (a skill folder before it is installed), check_mcp_config (a server definition before it is added) and explain_rule. It reads files only inside the folder it is started in, the folders given with --root and the roots the client shares. Speaks MCP 2026-07-28 and 2025-11-25.

Options:
  --root <folder>  Another folder the tools may read; repeat for more
  -h, --help       Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew mcp
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of scan 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew scan --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew scan: Check what steers agents in a project against the rules

Usage: agentcurfew scan [directory] [options]

Reads the skills, MCP servers, hooks, context files and permissions of a project, as the knowledge base describes them for each agent, and checks them against the rules. Nothing found is run, and nothing is sent anywhere. Exits with 1 when a finding is at --fail-on or above.

Options:
  --fail-on <level>  Exit with 1 at this level or above: critical, high (default), medium, low, none
  --json             Print JSON (see docs/cli-json.md); file contents and secrets are left out
  --sarif            Print SARIF 2.1.0, for GitHub Code Scanning and other tools
  --inventory        Only list the components, without the rules
  --user             Also read your home directory and machine settings
  -h, --help         Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew scan
  agentcurfew scan --fail-on medium
  agentcurfew scan --json
  agentcurfew scan --sarif
  agentcurfew scan --inventory
"
`;

exports[`screens > help of verify 1`] = `
"$ agentcurfew verify --help
[exit 0]
agentcurfew verify: Exit with 1 when a component changed since agentcurfew.lock

Usage: agentcurfew verify [directory] [options]

Compares the project with agentcurfew.lock and lists what was added, removed or changed. Also compares skills with skills-lock.json, which never changes the result.

Options:
  --json      Print JSON (see docs/cli-json.md); file contents and secrets are left out
  -h, --help  Show this help

Examples:
  agentcurfew verify
  agentcurfew verify --json
"
`;

